    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d535">

                <pb facs="0602" n="586" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg586"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.00B/9–1747:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Donnelly</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">San José</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-09-17T15:00:00-06:00">September 17,
                            1947—3 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>452. <hi rendition="#italic">La Tribuna</hi> today published text President
                    Picado’s press release referring to statements by opposition that Sept 15 parade
                    was “Communist demonstration for Communist chief of state”. The President
                    labeled it as malicious statement and added “the govt over which I preside is
                    not and never has been Communist” and said “the ideology and spirit of this
                    country is identified with the United States foreign <pb facs="0603" n="587"
                        xml:id="pg587"/> policy and with the democratic doctrines of the bloc of the
                    western nations as generally ref erred to”.</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">… Late yesterday afternoon President Picado asked me to call
                    on him. He reiterated his strong opposition to Communism and his loyalty to the
                    foreign policy of the U.S. Calderon Guardia sent word thru Dr. Anderson<anchor
                        n="15" xml:id="d535-f1"/> that he would welcome a meeting with me to inform
                    me of his views on Communism and his loyalty to U.S.…</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Donnelly</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d535-f1" n="15" xml:id="d535-f1-content">
                <p>Dr. Luis Anderson, Costa Rican statesman, lawyer, and professor.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
